export const body = "<h2>In The Mind of A Concept Artist</h2>\n\n<h2>How ideas become stories</h2>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<ul><li>Jamming for monsters!</li><li>The Sea Tick</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>Ideation</li><li>Drawing and modelling</li><li>Coloring and texturing</li><li>The aliasing incident</li><li>Photobashing</li><li>Final render</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>The Ghost of Fog</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>Ideation</li><li>Shape language</li><li>Fictional realism</li><li>Create then iterate</li><li>Visual storytelling</li><li>Everybody can use a good story</li><li>Final render</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>The Slithering Beast</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>Brainstorm</li><li>A hybrid concept</li><li>The best of both worlds</li><li>Previsualization</li><li>Final render</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>The Moonlight Dragon</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>Laying the groundwork</li><li>A process of elimination</li><li>(Almost) final render</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>Every piece of concept art is a story</li></ul>\n\n<h3>Jamming for monsters!</h3>\n\n<p>Between April and July of 2021, the studio organized an internal art contest called \"Monster Jam.\" This contest pairs one concept artist with one 3D character artist, then asks them to create an original monster design. The brief of Monster Jam reads:</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><em>This monster can be placed in the Godzilla or Pacific Rim world. It exists like a natural disaster. It has the strength to compete with other monsters for the title of 'The King of Monsters.'</em></p>\n<p><em>The body size is above 30 meters. It can live on the ground, under the sea, or up in the sky. And the visual direction must be realistic instead of stylized.</em></p></div>\n\n<p>One goal of Monster Jam is to spark a conversation between concept artists and 3D character artists. Concept artists get to understand the 3D character creation pipeline; 3D character artists get to learn the creative process of concept art.</p>\n\n\n<h3>The Sea Tick</h3>\n\n<h4>Ideation</h4>\n\n<p>Our first pair of winners is the artist (Concept Artist) and the artist (Character Artist).</p>\n\n<p>Initially, they planned to create a cute monster, drawing elements from the lizard and the mantis (because the artist really likes mantises). However, they quickly abandoned this idea when they realized that the creature couldn't grow 30 meters tall and, most importantly, look scary.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/80ecbf01b8f3eb425f4e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/80ecbf01b8f3eb425f4e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"380\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>After exploring many ideas, they arrived at a design primarily inspired by the tick. Leveraging his existing experience with monster modelling, the artist disassembled his old works, and then reassembled select parts of them to create the early 3D model in Blender.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/db9d97bab60e9bd64aed.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/db9d97bab60e9bd64aed.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"231\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>Drawing and modelling</h4>\n\n<p>Once the vision was set, the 2D artist continued to finalize his concept for the monster, and the 3D artist, polygon by polygon, materialized that monster in the 3D form.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/be8bc280e2b00890bf15.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/be8bc280e2b00890bf15.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"321\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>Coloring and texturing</h4>\n\n<p>As the artist carried out his modelling work, the artist further polished the concept by adding more details and colors. Below is the first color and texture pass of the creature. Though the texture work was quite rough during this stage, the artist said it was detailed enough to convey his intention. Besides, the environmental lighting in the final render was going to be fairly dark, meaning much of the creature's skin wouldn't be revealed anyway, he added.</p>\n\n<p>Another advantage of keeping the textures rough was the ease of iteration. The concept artist shared that if the textures had been too polished, it would've been difficult for him and his partner to make changes.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d54d00885e6554275b33.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d54d00885e6554275b33.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"189\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>The aliasing incident</h4>\n\n<p>In the initial render, the pair noticed the aliasing on the skin of the creature, which was unpleasant to look at. To resolve this issue, the artist exported the shadow, AO, and color layers of the texture to the artist, who then went into Photoshop to combine these layers together.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ef4b73b34c0e4ecaebb0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ef4b73b34c0e4ecaebb0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"384\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>Photobashing</h4>\n\n<p>As for the environment surrounding the monster, the artist collected a number of images of the hurricanes that took place in the West Coast of America. Then, he used the photobashing technique to blend them in with the 3D model of the sea monster.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0820f41a701119e1828d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0820f41a701119e1828d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"317\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>Final render</h4>\n\n<p>After 3 months of hard work, the two artists finally finished their gigantic sea monster. Have a look and enjoy!</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3618491e192e5c1dd292.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3618491e192e5c1dd292.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"620\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>The Ghost of Fog</h3>\n\n<h4>Ideation</h4>\n\n<p>The second creature comes from the collaboration between the artist (Concept Artist) and the artist (Character Artist).</p>\n\n<p>Before drawing his concept, the artist pondered on this question: \"Does this monster live on land? Or under the sea?\" Since the natural habitat of the monster would inform its physical characteristics, the answer to this question was going to be the artist’s creative compass for the whole design process.</p>\n\n<h4>Shape language</h4>\n\n<p>When designing a monster, the artist focuses on combining different shapes to express its personality. For the rough sketches, he would choose 1 or 2 shapes to establish the general flow in the creature's body. By limiting himself to 1 or 2 shapes, the artist can guarantee that the final product will have a consistent visual language. In this case, the concept artist decided to use mainly triangles.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/736d00eacdbd537850ef.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/736d00eacdbd537850ef.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"711\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>Fictional realism</h4>\n\n<p>Once the shape language was settled, the artist began to explore the anatomy of the monster. By mixing and matching components of various real-life creatures, he managed to produce a monster that looks unique, yet still grounded in reality.</p>\n\n<p>But the artist didn't stop at borrowing body parts. To blend these disparate elements seamlessly, he also exaggerated and rotated some of them so that they can fit into his established visual framework better.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/18a14473b80fc91fe12d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/18a14473b80fc91fe12d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"390\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>Create then iterate</h4>\n\n<p>Despite spending many hours putting together the previous design, the artist didn't feel connected to it. He felt like it didn't have a soul or a personality. For that reason, he decided to start over with a new direction.</p>\n\n<p>With the new creature, the artist made it his goal to give it a strong personality. When he began to work on this second design, he thought of something that would appear sacred like a guardian. Initially, the concept artist wasn't sure whether or not he should build this creature with a bipedal structure, for he was afraid a humanoid body would diminish the scare factor. But after much consideration, he was firm that the upstanding posture was a necessary decision to grant this being a sacred aura.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8d1a4c589c04bfe342f0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8d1a4c589c04bfe342f0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"326\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>Visual storytelling</h4>\n\n<p>During the design process, the artist did extensive research on mythological stories and apocalypses to refine his concept. In order to hint that this monster had been walking the earth for a long time, the concept artist turned to the source of ancient creatures: the ocean. Many people believe that life originated from the ocean eons ago, so when a monster carries oceanic characteristics, such as a seashell's texture and a shark tail, it gives off the impression of an ancient being emerging from the deepest of waters.</p>\n\n<p>Movies were another great source of inspiration, the concept artist said. The fog hair emanating along the back of the ghost was inspired by Peter Parker's Spider Sense, an ability that warns the user of imminent danger whenever his body hair stands up.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2bae2ba60182b3b4cee1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2bae2ba60182b3b4cee1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"445\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>Everybody can use a good story</h4>\n\n<p>The biggest difficulty in realizing this creature was the modelling step. Because of the elaborate design, there were several features that proved too complex to be represented precisely in 3D. At first, the artist was hesitant to sculpt the finer details on this creature because that process would take plenty of time. To convince the artist, the artist told the backstory he made for the creature, allowing his partner to understand why certain details were so important to keep.</p>\n\n<p>Inspired by the tale behind The Ghost of Fog, the artist became determined to bring this monster to life exactly as the artist had envisioned it.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ab1f175da2d5e84f37aa.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ab1f175da2d5e84f37aa.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"237\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>Final render</h4>\n\n<p>Together, the two artists told the story of a mysterious creature, one that furtively resides in the far reaches of a mountain forever embraced by endless streams of fog. In the final artwork, little human figures were added to emphasize the difference in scale between these two beings—one mortal, the other sacred—making The Ghost of Fog all the more imposing.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/88dd82003725fa2ee93a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/88dd82003725fa2ee93a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"649\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>The Slithering Beast</h3>\n\n<h4>Brainstorm</h4>\n\n<p>As we've learned so far, the beginning is the time that requires the most delicate care. Our third pair of winners, the artist (Concept Artist) and the artist (Character Artist) were tossing around many ideas before they settled with one. There were some sketches for a flying creature, some for creatures based on real-life animals, and some taking notes from Chinese mythology.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f7c8035558d8ce0ba7c6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f7c8035558d8ce0ba7c6.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"528\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>A hybrid concept</h4>\n\n<p>Eventually, they landed with the hybrid concept, twisting the human figure with characteristics of various animals. First, the claws below the waist of the monster were made with the seashell texture in mind. Covering the tail and the back of this creature is a set of reptilian scales. And finally, the artists add fins to select parts throughout the body to further evoke the aquatic nature of this concept.</p>\n\n<p>To help his partner with the texturing, Cheng Jianxian established a color scheme differentiating four texture types on this monster.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/97a6a129a16401a2a36d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/97a6a129a16401a2a36d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"611\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>The best of both worlds</h4>\n\n<p>When the artist began modelling the monster, he chose to alienate the design a bit to make the design look unworldly. This decision is most apparent in the head of the monster, which was comprised of rectangular shapes. Although the artist understood the direction his partner was taking, he felt like this iteration of the monster didn't belong to the earth. As a result, he suggested adding some earthly features to the head design such as the sharp teeth. Also, he recommended his partner taper it toward a triangular structure to better exhibit the aggressive quality of the monster.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c0e9391ab947ba9567d3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c0e9391ab947ba9567d3.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"303\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>After some discussion, they arrived at a middle ground that inherited the best of both worlds.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/04c2953ad36d24b5c93a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/04c2953ad36d24b5c93a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"863\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/cefec4c30c942cfe8fa7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/cefec4c30c942cfe8fa7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"863\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 18\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>Previsualization</h4>\n\n<p>Once the character model was agreed upon, the pair moved on to previsualization. During this stage, they explored several different environments, from ancient to modern cityscapes, to find out which one was the most suited to demonstrate their monster. The artist also used photobashing to integrate real photographs into their 2D image to develop their environment.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ebb2a7dfd0dc4f730d4a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ebb2a7dfd0dc4f730d4a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"222\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 19\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><br>Eventually, they settled on this modern city backdrop and the low camera angle. According to the artist, he finds this particular framing conveys the devastating impact of the monster most effectively.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/80e4cc3e99a51c798121.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/80e4cc3e99a51c798121.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 20\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>Final render</h4>\n\n<p>Using the contrast between the colossal creature and the tiny jets, between the advancing red horizon and the receding blue sky, compounded with the demolished landscape, the two artists were able to paint a terrifying picture of an unwavering force of nature.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/da78aff569a0caaa88bf.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/da78aff569a0caaa88bf.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"567\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 21\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>The Moonlight Dragon</h3>\n\n<h4>Laying the groundwork</h4>\n\n<p>Now is the time for our final pair of winners, the artist (Concept Artist) and the artist (Character Artist). From the first moment, these two artists had set out to create a monster design reminiscent of Eastern mythological creatures. One standout design was the stone lion whose head was populated with a cloudy pattern to express its Eastern origin. (A little piece of trivia: the elephant was the artist’s favorite design!)</p>\n\n<p>One noteworthy element in these sketches is the detailed linework and coloring. Knowing his partner was facing a tight schedule, the artist went the extra mile to polish his designs to provide a clear vision for 3D modelling.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9ee28158a02ae62ec16c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9ee28158a02ae62ec16c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"920\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 22\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>A process of elimination</h4>\n\n<p>After narrowing down the head designs, the concept artist proceeded with four full-body sketches.</p>\n\n<p>The first design on the top left is a fusion between the Chinese dragon and Godzilla. But this design was quickly abandoned because the pair was looking for a leaner creature, a monster with a delicate appearance.</p>\n\n<p>The second design elaborated on the six-armed elephant concept inspired by Buddhism. For this design, the artist tried to get rid of the religious association to better depict a monstrosity to be feared by mankind.</p>\n\n<p>Next up, we have an eight-headed snake, taking cues from the legendary serpent in Japanese folklore Yamata no Orochi. Initially, the artist intended to create a unique design for each head, but he soon realized that was too unnecessary a goal to accomplish. And so, the final design received eight similar-looking heads.</p>\n\n<p>Last but not least is a creature inspired by the hedgehog. A major creative decision the artist took with this design was the fiery bristles on its back, which added to the intimidating quality this monster already had on display.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e33bbbda9e736b20442b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e33bbbda9e736b20442b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"920\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 23\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Ultimately, the pair decided to move forward with the dragon concept, redesigning its skin and body structure to strike a good balance between Eastern and Western. By using traditional Chinese painting techniques, the artist seamlessly blended the dragon with the mountain on which it was perching. He was very pleased with this illustration.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/392d43ff4f5881faeff0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/392d43ff4f5881faeff0.webp\" width=\"756\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 24\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>(Almost) final render</h4>\n\n<p>Unfortunately, our final pair of winners ran short on time to complete their work, so they decided to envelop the creature with environmental details, such as the mountain ranges, the foreground trees, the thick fog, and of course the night sky. Though the artist wasn't able to drive this project to completion on time, he was satisfied with the environment he created for the final submission.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3d289f6dca1621458c9b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3d289f6dca1621458c9b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"500\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 25\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>However, the two artists said they will go on to finish this dragon concept (along with the elephant one). For now, you can have a look at the work-in-progress dragon model below.WIP models</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5df36f02f24cea7ed2e3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5df36f02f24cea7ed2e3.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"541\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 26\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/95463c158082c89ee734.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/95463c158082c89ee734.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"863\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 27\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Every piece of concept art is a story</h3>\n\n<p>Writers tell stories with nouns and verbs; artists tell stories with lines and shapes. Behind each of these gargantuan monsters is a tale waiting to be told.</p>\n\n<p>At first glance, Monster Jam is a fun activity that challenges the creativity of its participants. But beneath that premise is a bonding exercise between those who relish in the 2D realm and those whole revel in the 3D space. At the end of the event, our artists not only created some fantastical creatures but also gained stronger understanding of their partners' work process.</p>\n\n<p>We hope this story of Monster Jam inspires you. And we look forward to sharing more from all artists!</p>\n\n<p>Thank you for reading and happy creating!</p>";
export const images = ["assets/library-inline/04c2953ad36d24b5c93a.webp", "assets/library-inline/0820f41a701119e1828d.webp", "assets/library-inline/18a14473b80fc91fe12d.webp", "assets/library-inline/2bae2ba60182b3b4cee1.webp", "assets/library-inline/3618491e192e5c1dd292.webp", "assets/library-inline/392d43ff4f5881faeff0.webp", "assets/library-inline/3d289f6dca1621458c9b.webp", "assets/library-inline/5df36f02f24cea7ed2e3.webp", "assets/library-inline/736d00eacdbd537850ef.webp", "assets/library-inline/80e4cc3e99a51c798121.webp", "assets/library-inline/80ecbf01b8f3eb425f4e.webp", "assets/library-inline/88dd82003725fa2ee93a.webp", "assets/library-inline/8d1a4c589c04bfe342f0.webp", "assets/library-inline/95463c158082c89ee734.webp", "assets/library-inline/97a6a129a16401a2a36d.webp", "assets/library-inline/9ee28158a02ae62ec16c.webp", "assets/library-inline/ab1f175da2d5e84f37aa.webp", "assets/library-inline/be8bc280e2b00890bf15.webp", "assets/library-inline/c0e9391ab947ba9567d3.webp", "assets/library-inline/cefec4c30c942cfe8fa7.webp", "assets/library-inline/d54d00885e6554275b33.webp", "assets/library-inline/da78aff569a0caaa88bf.webp", "assets/library-inline/db9d97bab60e9bd64aed.webp", "assets/library-inline/e33bbbda9e736b20442b.webp", "assets/library-inline/ebb2a7dfd0dc4f730d4a.webp", "assets/library-inline/ef4b73b34c0e4ecaebb0.webp", "assets/library-inline/f7c8035558d8ce0ba7c6.webp"];
